            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Sanskrit for a son-in-law, then the Sanskrit for a daughter-in-law."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: सैनिकः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "सैनिकः — sainikaḥ — \"a soldier\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "sainika is built on senā, \"army\", with the suffix -ika, \"belonging to\" (MW): \"one who belongs to the army\". Senā is the old word for \"army\", and it lives on as Hindi sen, \"army\" (CDIAL 13587)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
